window.AILETMOCK={"n":7,"title":"Mock Test 7","theme":"Executive power and gender justice","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Reservation in promotions, executive power, office of profit, gender justice and the right to protest in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Reservation in Promotions","q":"In State of Kerala v. N.M. Thomas (1975), the Supreme Court held that Article 16(4):","o":["permits reservation only at the stage of first recruitment","is not an exception to Article 16(1) but a facet of equality","bars all reservation in promotion in every circumstance","is an exception to Article 16(1) and is to be read narrowly"],"a":1,"e":"The majority upheld a rule exempting SC and ST employees from a departmental test for promotion. Indra Sawhney (1992) later approved the same reading of Article 16(4), though it disallowed reservation in promotions until the 77th Amendment added Article 16(4A)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Executive Power","q":"In Ram Jawaya Kapur v. State of Punjab (1955), the Supreme Court held that the executive power of the State:","o":["is co-extensive with its legislative power, needing no law for each act","is limited to the matters in the Concurrent List alone","can be exercised only after a law has been passed on the subject in practice","extends only to carrying out the judgments of the courts"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that the executive may carry on trade or business, such as publishing textbooks, without specific legislation, but must act within the limits of the Constitution and the law. India has a parliamentary executive that is answerable to the legislature."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free Speech","q":"In Indian Express Newspapers (Bombay) Pvt. Ltd. v. Union of India (1985), the Supreme Court held that the press:","o":["is wholly immune from all kinds of taxes imposed by Parliament","is not immune from tax, but arbitrary levies curbing it are invalid","can be taxed only through ordinances issued by the President of India","can be taxed only if the newspaper's own Chairman has consented"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the press enjoys freedom under Article 19(1)(a) and that the duty on newsprint could be tested for arbitrariness and its effect on circulation. The Court looked at the impact of the tax on the freedom of the press."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Council of Ministers","q":"Under Articles 75(1A) and 164(1A), inserted by the 91st Amendment (2003), the total number of Ministers in a State Council of Ministers, including the Chief Minister, cannot exceed:","o":["15 per cent of the total members of the Legislative Assembly","20 per cent of the total members of the Legislative Assembly","10 per cent of the total members of the Legislative Council","25 per cent of the total members of the Legislative Assembly"],"a":0,"e":"For a State with a small Assembly, the minimum number of Ministers is twelve. At the Centre, the limit is 15 per cent of the total strength of the Lok Sabha."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Office of Profit","q":"In Jaya Bachchan v. Union of India (2006), the Supreme Court held, on 'office of profit' under Article 102(1)(a), that disqualification:","o":["arises only if the member has actually drawn some remuneration for it","turns on whether the office could yield profit, not on actual receipt","arises only if the Election Commission has notified the office earlier","arises only if the office is held under the Union Government alone"],"a":1,"e":"Jaya Bachchan was held disqualified as Chairperson of the U.P. Film Development Council. Parliament then passed the Parliament (Prevention of Disqualification) Amendment Act, 2006, which exempted a list of offices."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judicial Review","q":"In Maneka Gandhi and later cases, the test of 'arbitrariness' as a violation of Article 14 was first articulated in E.P. Royappa v. State of Tamil Nadu (1974), which held that arbitrariness is:","o":["relevant only to the exercise of power by the judiciary","relevant only to laws affecting the right to property","antithetical to equality, the two being sworn enemies","a part of Article 14 only for the legislative functions"],"a":2,"e":"Justice Bhagwati expressed a dynamic view of equality in Royappa, which was applied to State action generally. In Shayara Bano (2017), the Court applied manifest arbitrariness to strike down a statute."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Fundamental Rights","q":"In Behram Khurshid Pesikaka v. State of Bombay (1955), the Supreme Court held that Fundamental Rights which are conferred for public benefit:","o":["can be waived only by an affidavit sworn before a Magistrate","can be waived only in the case of the rights in Article 19","cannot be waived by an individual, as they rest on public policy","can be waived by any person at any time by his consent"],"a":2,"e":"Basheshar Nath v. CIT (1959) reaffirmed that Fundamental Rights cannot be waived. The principle protects citizens from being induced to give up constitutional protections."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 14","q":"In Ram Krishna Dalmia v. Justice S.R. Tendolkar (1958), the Supreme Court restated that there is a presumption that a law is:","o":["constitutional, and the burden is on the challenger to show a breach","unconstitutional wherever it singles out one person for treatment","unconstitutional until the State proves a reasonable classification","constitutional only after the President has approved it in writing"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that a law may be valid even if it applies to a single individual or institution, if there are special circumstances. The test of intelligible differentia and rational nexus is from State of West Bengal v. Anwar Ali Sarkar (1952)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Gender Justice","q":"In Anuj Garg v. Hotel Association of India (2007), the Supreme Court struck down a ban on women working in bars because:","o":["stereotyped protective discrimination violates Articles 14, 15 and 19(1)(g)","it denied men the opportunity of employment in bars and similar places","it was passed without the assent of the President of India, as required","it infringed the bar owners' freedom of contract under Article 300A"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that such paternalism perpetuates gender stereotypes and that the law should aim to remove obstacles that women face, not restrict their choices."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free Speech","q":"In Bennett Coleman & Co. v. Union of India (1973), the Supreme Court struck down the newsprint policy because it:","o":["fell outside the power of the Union over foreign trade","violated the newspapers' right to property under Article 31","violated Article 19(1)(a) by restricting pages and circulation","violated Article 14 by favouring English newspapers over regional ones"],"a":2,"e":"The Court looked at the direct and inevitable effect of the policy on the freedom of the press. In Sakal Papers (1962), the Court had similarly struck down a price-page schedule for newspapers."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Protest","q":"In Ramlila Maidan Incident, In re (2012), the Supreme Court held that the midnight police action against sleeping protesters violated:","o":["only Article 32, as the protesters had not approached the High Court","only Article 14, and no other Fundamental Right of the protesters","only Article 19(1)(a), since sleep is not covered by Article 21","the rights to assemble peacefully and to sleep, under Articles 19 and 21"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the right to sleep is a part of the right to life, and that the right to protest is subject to reasonable restrictions. In Amit Sahni (2020), the Court held that public ways cannot be occupied indefinitely for protests."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Dignity","q":"In Francis Coralie Mullin v. Administrator, Union Territory of Delhi (1981), the Supreme Court held that the right to life under Article 21 includes:","o":["only the right to be free from physical torture by a public official","only the right to bare animal existence and survival of the person","only the right to a fair trial before a competent court of law","the right to live with human dignity, including adequate nutrition and shelter"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that a detenu is entitled to meet family members and lawyers under reasonable conditions. The case widened Article 21 beyond mere survival."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Prison Rights","q":"In Sunil Batra v. Delhi Administration (1978), where a prisoner's letter was treated as a writ petition, the Supreme Court held that:","o":["convicts retain Fundamental Rights, and needless solitary confinement violates Article 21","solitary confinement is valid for every prisoner who is under a sentence of death on the facts","courts cannot examine the conditions inside prisons in any manner whatsoever","convicts lose all of their Fundamental Rights on conviction by a competent court"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that prisoners are not non-persons and that the court has a 'hands-on' role in protecting them. The case is an example of epistolary jurisdiction."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Religious Freedom","q":"In Commissioner, Hindu Religious Endowments v. Sri Lakshmindra Thirtha Swamiar (Shirur Mutt, 1954), the Supreme Court held that Article 25 protects:","o":["only practices followed by a majority of a religious group","only practices approved by the State Government concerned","only religious beliefs, and not the practices of any religion in practice","essential religious practices, and not merely doctrines or beliefs"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that what constitutes an essential part of a religion is to be decided by the doctrines of that religion itself. Secular activities connected with religion may still be regulated by the State."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Vice-President","q":"Under Article 89, the Vice-President, as ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha, has:","o":["no vote of any kind, as he is not a member of the House","a casting vote only, in case of an equality of votes","a casting vote in a joint sitting held under Article 108","an original vote in every division of the House, as a member"],"a":1,"e":"The Vice-President is not a member of either House. A joint sitting is presided over by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, and not by the Vice-President."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliament","q":"Under Article 85, the interval between the last sitting of Parliament in one session and its first sitting in the next session cannot exceed:","o":["three months","nine months","twelve months","six months"],"a":3,"e":"Parliament must therefore meet at least twice a year. The President summons the Houses and may prorogue them on the advice of the Council of Ministers."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliament","q":"Under Article 94, a resolution for the removal of the Speaker of the Lok Sabha needs fourteen days' notice and must be passed by:","o":["two-thirds of the members present and voting in the House","a majority of all the then members of the House","a majority of the members present and voting in the House","two-thirds of the total membership of the House"],"a":1,"e":"Under Article 96, the Speaker cannot preside while his removal resolution is being considered, but may speak and vote in the first instance (not in the case of a tie)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Finance","q":"Under Article 114, no money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund of India except under:","o":["a notification of the Ministry of Finance","an appropriation made by law","a resolution of the Lok Sabha alone","an order of the Comptroller and Auditor General"],"a":1,"e":"The Appropriation Bill authorises the expenditure. Article 112 provides for the Annual Financial Statement, Article 113 for demands for grants, and Article 116 for a vote on account."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Constitutional Offices","q":"Under Article 165, the Advocate-General of a State is appointed by:","o":["the President, on the advice of the Chief Justice of India","the State Legislature, on a motion moved in the Assembly","the Governor, from among persons qualified as High Court judges","the Chief Minister, from the members of the State Bar Council"],"a":2,"e":"The Advocate-General holds office during the pleasure of the Governor and has the right to speak in the State Legislature, though not to vote."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judiciary","q":"Under Article 233, the appointment of district judges in a State is made by:","o":["the Chief Justice of the High Court, after consulting the Governor","the Governor, in consultation with the High Court in such cases","the State Public Service Commission, with the Governor's approval","the President, in consultation with the Chief Justice of India"],"a":1,"e":"In Chandra Mohan v. State of U.P. (1966), the Supreme Court held that the consultation with the High Court is mandatory, and that persons in the judicial service alone, or advocates, are eligible, and not those in the executive service."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Finance Commission","q":"Under Article 280, the Finance Commission is constituted by the President:","o":["every second year, to review the Union Budget","at the end of every fifth year, or earlier if needed","whenever the Lok Sabha passes a resolution requesting it","every tenth year, after the decennial Census"],"a":1,"e":"The Commission recommends the distribution of net tax proceeds between the Union and the States. The 16th Finance Commission, chaired by Arvind Panagariya, was constituted in December 2023. (Verify its report and award period.)"},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"All-India Services","q":"Under Article 312, Parliament may create a new All-India Service if the Rajya Sabha passes a resolution supported by:","o":["a simple majority of the members present and voting in the House","a majority of the State Legislatures, passing similar resolutions","two-thirds of members present and voting, in the national interest","two-thirds of the total membership of the Rajya Sabha"],"a":2,"e":"The IAS, IPS and Indian Forest Service are the existing All-India Services. The Rajya Sabha is given this role, as it represents the States."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Elections","q":"The age of voting for elections to the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies was lowered from 21 to 18 years by which Amendment?","o":["73rd Amendment, 1992","44th Amendment, 1978","61st Amendment, 1988","42nd Amendment, 1976"],"a":2,"e":"The amendment changed Article 326. The Court has held that the right to vote is a statutory right, though universal adult suffrage is a basic feature."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Preamble","q":"In Dr. Balram Singh v. Union of India (2024), the Supreme Court rejected a challenge to the insertion of 'socialist' and 'secular' in the Preamble by the 42nd Amendment, holding that:","o":["the words could be retained only by a fresh ratification of States","the Preamble cannot be amended, as it is not part of the Constitution","the words can be added only after a national referendum is held","Parliament's amending power under Article 368 extends to the Preamble"],"a":3,"e":"The Court relied on Kesavananda Bharati (1973) and S.R. Bommai (1994), and noted that the words have acquired a settled meaning over the years. The petitioners argued that the Preamble's adoption date of 26 November 1949 could not be altered."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Sexual Offences","q":"In Independent Thought v. Union of India (2017), the Supreme Court read down the exception to rape (now Exception 2 to Section 63 of the BNS) to hold that sexual intercourse with a wife is rape if she is:","o":["below twenty-one years of age","below sixteen years of age","below fifteen years of age","below eighteen years of age"],"a":3,"e":"The earlier exception covered wives above fifteen, which conflicted with the POCSO Act and the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act. The exception for adult wives remains. (Verify the status of the challenges to it.)"},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 70(1) of the BNS, gang rape of a woman above eighteen years of age is punishable with rigorous imprisonment of:","o":["not less than 10 years, which may extend to 20 years","not less than 20 years, extendable to life, and fine","not less than 14 years, which may extend to death","not less than 7 years, which may extend to 10 years"],"a":1,"e":"Life imprisonment means the remainder of the person's natural life. Gang rape of a woman under eighteen years is punishable under Section 70(2) with life imprisonment or death."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Stalking under Section 78 of the BNS, on a second or subsequent conviction, is punishable with imprisonment up to:","o":["five years, and fine","two years, and fine","seven years, and fine","three years, and fine"],"a":0,"e":"On a first conviction, the punishment is up to three years and fine. Stalking includes monitoring a woman's use of the internet or electronic communication."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Dowry death under Section 80(2) of the BNS, corresponding to Section 304B of the IPC, is punishable with imprisonment of not less than:","o":["three years, which may extend to seven years","ten years, which may extend to fourteen years","seven years, which may extend to life","five years, which may extend to ten years"],"a":2,"e":"It requires death within seven years of marriage in unnatural circumstances, and cruelty or harassment for dowry soon before death. Section 118 of the BSA gives a presumption."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Section 105 of the BNS punishes culpable homicide not amounting to murder, where the act is done with the intention of causing death, with:","o":["imprisonment for life only, with no lesser term of years","imprisonment up to three years, or fine, or both","imprisonment for life, or five to ten years, and fine","imprisonment of two to five years, and fine only"],"a":2,"e":"Where the act is done with only the knowledge that it is likely to cause death, the punishment is imprisonment up to ten years, or fine, or both."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 310(2) of the BNS, dacoity is punishable with:","o":["life imprisonment, or rigorous imprisonment up to ten years, and fine","imprisonment for life only, with no lesser option available","rigorous imprisonment up to fourteen years, and fine as well on the facts","rigorous imprisonment up to seven years, and fine as well"],"a":0,"e":"Dacoity under Section 310(1) means robbery committed by five or more persons conjointly. If the offence involves murder, the punishment is death, life imprisonment or rigorous imprisonment of at least ten years."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Cheating and dishonestly inducing delivery of property, under Section 318(4) of the BNS, is punishable with imprisonment up to:","o":["five years, and fine","three years, and fine","seven years, and fine","ten years, and fine"],"a":2,"e":"Simple cheating under Section 318(2) carries up to three years. The IPC counterpart of Section 318(4) was Section 420."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 173(3) of the BNSS, for a cognizable offence punishable with three years or more but less than seven years, the officer in charge may, with permission, hold a preliminary enquiry within:","o":["seven days","fourteen days","thirty days","sixty days"],"a":1,"e":"Permission must come from an officer not below the rank of Deputy Superintendent of Police. In Lalita Kumari (2014), the Court allowed a preliminary enquiry in specified cases to be completed within seven days."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under the BNSS, the police must inform the informant or the victim of the progress of an investigation within:","o":["one hundred and eighty days, by public notice","thirty days, only through a written letter","sixty days, only through the Magistrate's office","ninety days, including by electronic means"],"a":3,"e":"This is in Section 193(3)(ii). In offences of rape, the investigation must be completed within two months under Section 193(2)."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 105 of the BNSS, the process of search and seizure of premises must be:","o":["carried out only at night, in the presence of two senior officers","witnessed only by a police officer above the rank of Inspector","approved in advance by a Sessions Judge in every case of search","recorded by audio-video means, preferably on a mobile phone"],"a":3,"e":"The recording must be sent without delay to the District Magistrate or the Judicial Magistrate. The provision aims to improve the credibility of seizures."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"In Pulukuri Kottaya v. Emperor (1947), the Privy Council held that under Section 27 of the Evidence Act (now Section 23 of the BSA), the admissible part of an accused's statement to the police is that which:","o":["contains a full confession made by the accused to the police","distinctly relates to the fact discovered through the information","relates to the motive of the crime and the accused's intention","is recorded by a Magistrate and signed by the accused person"],"a":1,"e":"The section is a proviso to the bar on confessions made to the police. The discovery must be of a fact, and the information must have led to it."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Death Penalty","q":"In Machhi Singh v. State of Punjab (1983), the Supreme Court elaborated the 'rarest of rare' doctrine of Bachan Singh by laying down:","o":["a rule that death is the normal punishment for murder","a fixed minimum term of fourteen years for life convicts","five categories of circumstances, such as manner and motive","a rule that death is barred where the accused is below forty"],"a":2,"e":"The categories are manner of commission, motive, anti-social or abhorrent nature, magnitude, and the personality of the victim. The Court asked for a balance of aggravating and mitigating circumstances."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Sexual Offences","q":"The Supreme Court's decision in Tuka Ram v. State of Maharashtra (the Mathura rape case, 1979) led to the 1983 amendment which inserted:","o":["a requirement of corroboration for the survivor's own evidence","a presumption of absence of consent in some custodial rape cases","a rule that the survivor's past conduct is proof of consent","a mandatory death sentence for every conviction of rape"],"a":1,"e":"The 1983 amendment inserted Section 114A of the Evidence Act (now Section 120 of the BSA), custodial rape as an aggravated offence, and in-camera trials for rape."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Law Reform","q":"Which Committee, constituted after the December 2012 Delhi gang-rape, made recommendations that led to the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2013?","o":["the Justice Malimath Committee","the Justice J.S. Verma Committee","the Justice Madhava Menon Committee","the Justice Mulla Committee"],"a":1,"e":"The 2013 Act added acid attack, stalking, voyeurism and sexual harassment as offences, and widened the definition of rape. The Malimath Committee (2003) dealt with criminal justice reforms."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Damages","q":"In Hadley v. Baxendale (1854), reflected in Section 73 of the Indian Contract Act, damages for breach of contract are recoverable for loss that:","o":["arises naturally from the breach, or was in contemplation","was caused by the breach and the plaintiff's own default","arises only if the contract has a liquidated damages clause","was suffered by the plaintiff, however remote it may have been"],"a":0,"e":"The mill-shaft case laid down the rule of remoteness. Loss that is special must have been communicated to the defendant when the contract was made."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Formation of Contract","q":"In Bhagwandas Goverdhandas Kedia v. Girdharilal Parshottamdas (1966), the Supreme Court held that a contract made over the telephone is formed at:","o":["the place where the acceptance is heard by the offeror","the place from where the acceptor speaks the acceptance","the place where the offeror lives or works at the time","a place that the parties fix after the call has ended"],"a":0,"e":"The postal rule does not apply to instantaneous communications such as the telephone or telex. The rule follows Entores v. Miles Far East (1955)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Minors","q":"In Mohori Bibee v. Dharmodas Ghose (1903), the Privy Council held that an agreement with a minor is:","o":["valid, in every case where it is for the minor's benefit","valid, if the minor ratifies it on attaining majority","void ab initio, so money advanced to him cannot be recovered","voidable at the minor's option, after he attains majority"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 11, a minor is not competent to contract. A minor cannot ratify an agreement after attaining majority, and Section 65 does not apply, but a supplier of necessaries may claim from the minor's property under Section 68."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Undue Influence","q":"Under Section 16(3) of the Contract Act, where a person in a position to dominate another's will obtains a contract that appears unconscionable, the burden of proving that undue influence was not used lies on:","o":["the person in the position to dominate the will","the court, which must inquire into it on its own","the weaker party, who must prove that it was used","the witness who has attested the contract, if any"],"a":0,"e":"The presumption arises where the relationship is such that one party can dominate the other, such as parent and child, doctor and patient, or solicitor and client."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Time of the Essence","q":"Under Section 55 of the Contract Act, where time is of the essence and a party fails to perform by the specified time, the contract:","o":["remains fully enforceable, as time is ignored by law","stands discharged only by a decree of a court","becomes voidable at the option of the promisee","becomes void, without any option to the promisee"],"a":2,"e":"In contracts for sale of immovable property, time is not presumed to be of the essence unless the parties clearly intend so. Courts look at the contract's terms and the surrounding circumstances."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"The rule in Rylands v. Fletcher (1868) imposes liability, without proof of negligence, on a person who:","o":["uses his land in a reasonable manner that is customary in the area","creates a nuisance as a result of the act of a stranger","causes damage to a neighbour's land with the neighbour's consent","accumulates a dangerous thing for non-natural use, and it escapes"],"a":3,"e":"The rule has defences such as the plaintiff's consent, the act of a stranger, act of God and statutory authority. In India, it was replaced by absolute liability for hazardous industries in M.C. Mehta (1987)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Negligence","q":"The three-stage test for a duty of care in negligence, laid down in Caparo Industries v. Dickman (1990), requires:","o":["intention to harm, causation, and an actual money loss suffered","a contract, consideration, and a breach of the promise made","remoteness, voluntary assumption, and a statutory duty of care","foreseeability, proximity, and that it be fair, just and reasonable"],"a":3,"e":"The House of Lords moved away from the wide neighbour principle of Donoghue v. Stevenson, taking an incremental approach to new categories of duty."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"To succeed in an action for malicious prosecution, the plaintiff must prove that the prosecution:","o":["ended in his favour, without reasonable cause, and with malice","ended in a compromise between the parties, with no damage to him","was instituted by a public servant only, with the leave of the court","was instituted only after the period of limitation had already expired"],"a":0,"e":"The plaintiff must also prove damage to his reputation, person or property. It is the tort of abusing the criminal process."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Occupiers' Liability","q":"Under the common law rule in Indermaur v. Dames (1866), an occupier owes the highest duty of care to:","o":["a trespasser, who enters the premises without any permission in practice","a child trespasser, in all of the circumstances of the case","an invitee, who comes for the business or benefit of the occupier","a licensee, who comes for his own social purposes alone"],"a":2,"e":"The invitee must be protected against unusual dangers which the occupier knows of or ought to know of. In England, the Occupiers' Liability Act, 1957 replaced these categories with a common duty of care."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Under Section 6(a) of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, the chance of an heir apparent succeeding to an estate, known as spes successionis:","o":["can be transferred with the consent of the testator","cannot be transferred","can be transferred only to a blood relative of the heir","can be transferred by a registered deed only"],"a":1,"e":"Section 6 also bars the transfer of a mere right to sue, a public office, and a right of re-entry on its own. The heir has only an expectation, which is not property."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"The rule against perpetuity in Section 14 of the Transfer of Property Act bars a transfer under which an interest vests:","o":["later than lives in being plus the minority of a person then in being","within the lifetime of the transferor, without any extension at all","after twenty-one years from the death of the transferor, and not before","later than twelve years from the date of the transfer of the property"],"a":0,"e":"In India, the period is the life of one or more persons living at the date of the transfer, plus the minority of a person who would be in existence at the end of that period. The rule does not apply to charitable gifts for public benefit."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Under the doctrine of election in Section 35 of the Transfer of Property Act, a person who accepts a benefit under an instrument:","o":["may keep the benefit if he pays a fee fixed by the court","must also accept the burdens imposed by the same instrument","loses the benefit if he is a minor at the time of transfer","may keep the benefit and refuse every burden attached to it"],"a":1,"e":"The doctrine reflects the principle that a person cannot approbate and reprobate. A person cannot take under an instrument and also against it."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Muslim Law","q":"Under Muslim law, 'mehr' (dower) is:","o":["a gift voluntarily made by the wife's father to the husband","an obligation of the husband to pay a sum to the wife on marriage","a sum payable only if the marriage ends by the husband's death","a price paid for the marriage, to be returned on a divorce"],"a":1,"e":"Mehr may be prompt or deferred, and a wife may refuse cohabitation until the prompt dower is paid. It is an obligation imposed by law on the husband and not a consideration for the marriage."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Positivism","q":"H.L.A. Hart's 'minimum content of natural law' holds that:","o":["a surviving legal system must protect persons, property and promises","any valid law must satisfy a moral test before it can bind anyone","law is the command of the sovereign backed by the threat of sanction","law is whatever the judges say it is, in the case before them"],"a":0,"e":"Hart based this on facts of human vulnerability, approximate equality, limited altruism, limited resources and limited understanding. He remained a legal positivist, as the link is contingent and not conceptual."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Historical School","q":"Sir Henry Maine, in 'Ancient Law', described the movement of progressive societies as a movement:","o":["from custom to command","from reason to revelation","from status to contract","from contract to status"],"a":2,"e":"Maine, of the historical school, observed that in early societies a person's rights depended on his family status, and in progressive societies on agreements freely made."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Sociological School","q":"Rudolf von Jhering, in 'Law as a Means to an End', viewed law as:","o":["an expression of the spirit of the people over a period of time","a means of securing social interests, backed by State coercion","a command of a determinate sovereign to a political inferior","a set of timeless principles that are found through reason"],"a":1,"e":"Jhering, a pioneer of sociological jurisprudence, saw law as a conscious instrument for achieving social purposes, and wrote of the 'struggle for law'."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Legal Realism","q":"Jerome Frank, a legal realist, is called a 'fact-sceptic' because he held that:","o":["legal rules are always vague and cannot guide any judge at all","facts are never relevant to the legal decision of a case at all","judges decide cases only by applying the facts to the precedent","uncertain trial-court fact-finding makes outcomes unpredictable"],"a":3,"e":"Karl Llewellyn is classed as a rule-sceptic. Frank argued that the personality of the judge and the jury, and witness reliability, shape the decision."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'nemo dat quod non habet' means that:","o":["no one is bound to accuse himself of a wrong","no one can be punished twice for the same offence","no one can be a judge in his own cause","no one can give what he does not have"],"a":3,"e":"It is the basis of Section 27 of the Sale of Goods Act, 1930, under which a buyer generally gets no better title than the seller had, subject to exceptions such as sale by a mercantile agent."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'qui facit per alium facit per se' means that:","o":["he who benefits from an act must also bear its loss","he who acts for himself acts through no other person","he who acts through another is deemed to act himself","he who pays another is not liable for his acts at all"],"a":2,"e":"The maxim is the basis of the law of agency and the principle of vicarious liability."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'sic utere tuo ut alienum non laedas', the basis of the law of nuisance, means:","o":["use another's property only with his consent in writing","own property only if you pay all of the taxes due on it","use your property in any manner that you wish to use it","use your own property so as not to injure another's property"],"a":3,"e":"It underlies the law of nuisance and the principle of absolute liability, but a lawful use that causes no legal injury is not actionable."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'actio personalis moritur cum persona' means that:","o":["a personal action must be brought within three years","a personal right of action dies with the person","a right of action passes to the heirs of the person","an action lies only against a natural person and not a company"],"a":1,"e":"Indian law has limited the maxim. Under Section 306 of the Indian Succession Act, 1925, causes of action survive, except for defamation, assault and other personal injuries not causing damage to the estate."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The principle 'in dubio pro reo' means that:","o":["in case of doubt, the decision must favour the accused","in case of doubt, the decision must favour the complainant","in case of doubt, the decision must favour the State","in case of doubt, the court must order a fresh trial"],"a":0,"e":"It is linked to the presumption of innocence and the rule that the prosecution must prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt, and the benefit of doubt goes to the accused."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"A tenant claims that a statute should not be read to deprive him of a vested right unless the words clearly say so. Which interpretive presumption does he rely on?","o":["Presumption in favour of delegated legislation","Presumption that the legislature has used surplusage","Presumption against retrospective operation of statutes","Presumption that every statute is penal in nature"],"a":2,"e":"Statutes are presumed to operate prospectively, and vested rights are not taken away by implication unless the legislature clearly intends it (see Article 20(1) for penal laws and the general rule of construction)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Precedent","q":"A decision of the Supreme Court rendered 'per incuriam' is one which:","o":["is given by a bench of two judges, and not by a bench of three","is given in ignorance of a binding statute or earlier precedent","is given without hearing the counsel of the State at all","is given by a majority and not by a unanimous bench"],"a":1,"e":"Such a decision is not binding as a precedent. The doctrine comes from Young v. Bristol Aeroplane Co. (1944)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The rule of 'harmonious construction' requires that, where two provisions of a statute seem to conflict, a court should:","o":["refer the conflict to the legislature to resolve by amendment in practice","give effect to the earlier provision and ignore the later one","interpret them so as to give effect to both, avoiding any conflict","strike down the one that appears later in the statute in such cases"],"a":2,"e":"The rule is based on the maxim 'ut res magis valeat quam pereat'. In Venkataramana Devaru v. State of Mysore (1958), the Court reconciled Article 25(2)(b) with Article 26(b)."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICJ Advisory Opinions","q":"In its 2019 Advisory Opinion on the Chagos Archipelago, the ICJ held that the United Kingdom was under an obligation to:","o":["end its administration of the Archipelago as rapidly as possible","restore the pre-1965 boundary between Mauritius and the Seychelles","pay damages to Mauritius for the loss of fisheries in the area","hold a referendum among the residents of the island of Diego Garcia"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that decolonisation of Mauritius was not lawfully completed in 1968, as the detachment of the Archipelago violated the right to self-determination. The General Assembly endorsed the opinion in 2019, and India voted in favour. (Verify the 2024 UK-Mauritius agreement.)"},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Climate Law","q":"In its Advisory Opinion on the Obligations of States in respect of Climate Change (July 2025), the ICJ held that:","o":["only developed States are bound by obligations on climate change","climate change is not within the scope of international law at all","States have no binding duty beyond the goals of the Paris Agreement","States have binding duties under treaties and custom on climate"],"a":3,"e":"The Court said that a failure of a State to take appropriate action may be an internationally wrongful act, with legal consequences of cessation and reparation. (Verify the details before the exam.)"},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICJ Jurisdiction","q":"In The Gambia v. Myanmar (2022), the ICJ rejected Myanmar's preliminary objections and held that The Gambia, though not directly injured, had standing because the Genocide Convention creates obligations:","o":["jus cogens only, owed to the UN Security Council alone","erga omnes partes, owed to all the States parties","erga omnes only, owed to the States of the same region","inter partes only, owed to the injured State alone"],"a":1,"e":"The case concerned the Rohingya, and the Court had earlier indicated provisional measures in January 2020. Any State party may invoke the responsibility of another for breach of the Convention."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"United Nations","q":"The UN Human Rights Council, established in 2006, replaced which earlier body?","o":["the Economic and Social Council","the Committee against Torture","the Trusteeship Council","the Commission on Human Rights"],"a":3,"e":"The Council is a subsidiary organ of the General Assembly, with 47 members. It runs the Universal Periodic Review and a system of Special Procedures."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights Treaties","q":"The Supreme Court relied on CEDAW (1979) in Vishaka (1997) because:","o":["it was enacted as an Act of Parliament in India in the year 1980","it overrides the Constitution wherever the two happen to conflict","it applies only to States that have ratified its Optional Protocol on the facts","consistent international norms can be read into Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21"],"a":3,"e":"India ratified CEDAW in 1993. The Court relied on Articles 51(c) and 253 to fill the legislative gap on sexual harassment at the workplace until the POSH Act, 2013."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights Treaties","q":"India has signed but not ratified which of the following international instruments?","o":["the Convention against Torture, 1984 in the circumstances","the Convention on the Rights of the Child, 1989","the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, 1966","the Convention on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination, 1965"],"a":0,"e":"India signed the UN Convention against Torture in 1997. The Prevention of Torture Bill, 2010 lapsed, and the Law Commission's 273rd Report (2017) recommended ratification. (Verify the current position.)"},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Law of the Sea","q":"In the Enrica Lexie case (Italy v. India), the UNCLOS Annex VII Arbitral Tribunal held in 2020 that:","o":["India had exclusive jurisdiction to try the marines in its own courts","Italy was entitled to damages for the detention of its marines","the incident was outside the EEZ and so UNCLOS did not apply in practice","the Italian marines enjoy immunity, but India is entitled to compensation"],"a":3,"e":"The incident of February 2012 off Kerala caused the death of two fishermen. In 2021, the Supreme Court of India closed the proceedings after Italy deposited compensation."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Trade Law","q":"The Doha Declaration on the TRIPS Agreement and Public Health (2001) affirmed that the TRIPS Agreement:","o":["bars every compulsory licence for pharmaceutical products in all States","applies only to the least developed countries of the world at large","requires a patent term of thirty years on all of the medicines","allows measures to protect public health, including compulsory licences"],"a":3,"e":"It supports Section 84 of the Indian Patents Act. The first compulsory licence in India was granted to Natco for Bayer's sorafenib in 2012."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Investment Law","q":"In Cairn Energy v. India (PCA, 2020), the tribunal held that India's retrospective tax demand under the 2012 amendment breached:","o":["the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969, on reservations","Article 2(4) of the Charter of the United Nations on use of force","the fair and equitable treatment standard of the UK-India treaty","Article 27 of the TRIPS Agreement on the patentability of inventions"],"a":2,"e":"India later repealed the retrospective tax by the Taxation Laws (Amendment) Act, 2021 and offered to refund the tax collected."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Trade Marks","q":"Under Section 2(1)(zg) of the Trade Marks Act, 1999, a 'well-known trade mark' is one which is known to a substantial segment of the public such that:","o":["it has been registered in at least three other countries","it has been used continuously for more than fifty years","it has been declared so by the Central Government","its use on other goods suggests a connection with its owner"],"a":3,"e":"The Registrar or a court determines well-known status by looking at factors listed in Section 11(6) to (9). Tata Sons v. Manoj Dodia (Delhi High Court, 2011) is a leading case."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"In Eastern Book Company v. D.B. Modak (2008), the Supreme Court adopted which standard of originality for copyright in works such as copy-edited law reports?","o":["a modicum of creativity","pure novelty","artistic merit","sweat of the brow"],"a":0,"e":"The Court rejected the 'sweat of the brow' approach and also the high creativity test, and held that the headnotes and arrangement involved a minimum degree of creativity."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"Under Section 22 of the Copyright Act, 1957, copyright in a published literary work lasts for:","o":["the life of the author, plus sixty years from the next calendar year","the life of the author, plus fifty years, counted from the next year","a fixed period of sixty years from first publication of the work alone","the life of the author, plus seventy years, counted from the next year"],"a":0,"e":"India follows a term of life plus sixty years. The European Union and the United States follow life plus seventy years."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Patents","q":"Under Section 6 of the Patents Act, 1970, an application for a patent may be made by:","o":["only a citizen of India who is resident in the country at the time","the first person to publish the invention in a recognised journal","the true and first inventor, his assignee or legal representative","any person who has paid the prescribed filing fee to the Controller"],"a":2,"e":"The Act uses the term 'true and first inventor', and the Controller examines the application. India follows the first-to-file system with the priority date of the application."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Designs","q":"Under the Designs Act, 2000, copyright in a registered design subsists initially for:","o":["fifteen years, which cannot be renewed","five years, extendable by five years","ten years, extendable by five years","twenty years, which cannot be renewed"],"a":2,"e":"The registered proprietor may seek the extension before the initial period ends. A design must be new or original and not previously published."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Cyber Law","q":"Tampering with computer source documents under Section 65 of the IT Act, 2000 is punishable with imprisonment up to three years, or fine up to:","o":["₹2 lakh, or both","₹10 lakh, or both","₹5 lakh, or both","₹50,000, or both"],"a":0,"e":"Section 66 deals with computer-related offences done dishonestly or fraudulently, and Section 66F with cyber terrorism."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Securities Law","q":"Under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015, 'unpublished price sensitive information' is information that:","o":["relates only to a merger between two listed companies in India","is held only by the promoters of the listed company concerned","is made public only after the close of trading hours on a given day","is not generally available and may materially affect the price"],"a":3,"e":"Examples include financial results, dividends, changes in capital structure and mergers. An insider who trades while in possession of such information violates the regulations."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Negotiable Instruments","q":"Section 143A of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881, inserted in 2018, empowers the court trying a cheque dishonour case to order the drawer to pay interim compensation of up to:","o":["20 per cent of the amount of the cheque","50 per cent of the amount of the cheque","10 per cent of the amount of the cheque","30 per cent of the amount of the cheque"],"a":0,"e":"Section 148 allows an appellate court to direct the appellant to deposit at least 20 per cent of the fine or compensation. Section 138 prescribes imprisonment up to two years or a fine up to twice the cheque amount."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Company Law","q":"A company incorporated under Section 8 of the Companies Act, 2013 is one formed to promote:","o":["promotion of commerce, art, science and charity, with profits applied to them","insurance business that is regulated by the IRDAI alone, under its own Act","private speculation, with the profits shared among all of the members","public financial intermediation, with a licence from the RBI alone, to be issued"],"a":0,"e":"It needs a licence from the Central Government, cannot pay dividends to members, and may omit 'Limited' or 'Private Limited' from its name."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Insolvency","q":"In Committee of Creditors of Essar Steel India Ltd. v. Satish Kumar Gupta (2019), the Supreme Court held that the commercial wisdom of the Committee of Creditors:","o":["may be overridden by the Adjudicating Authority at its will","may be reviewed by the NCLT on the ground of fairness","is not justiciable and is beyond judicial review on its merits","is subject to approval by the Reserve Bank of India"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the role of the Adjudicating Authority is limited to checking compliance with the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, and that the equal treatment of creditors in the same class is required."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Data Protection","q":"Under the Schedule to the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, the maximum penalty for failing to take reasonable security safeguards to prevent a personal data breach is:","o":["₹500 crore","₹100 crore","₹50 crore","₹250 crore"],"a":3,"e":"Failure to notify a breach, or breach of children's data obligations, attracts up to ₹200 crore. The Data Protection Board decides, and appeals go to the TDSAT."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Judicial Review","q":"In Council of Civil Service Unions v. Minister for the Civil Service (GCHQ, 1985), Lord Diplock classified the grounds of judicial review as:","o":["illegality, proportionality and legitimate expectation","illegality, irrationality and procedural impropriety","irrationality, bias and delegation of power","jurisdiction, mala fides and ultra vires action"],"a":1,"e":"Lord Diplock said proportionality might be added in future. 'Irrationality' means Wednesbury unreasonableness."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Clearance","q":"Under the Environmental Impact Assessment Notification, 2006, projects listed as Category A are appraised by:","o":["an Expert Appraisal Committee of the Central Government","the Gram Sabha of the village where the project is sited","the State Pollution Control Board, with no further appraisal","the National Green Tribunal, before any project begins"],"a":0,"e":"Category B projects are appraised at the State level by a State Environment Impact Assessment Authority. Prior environmental clearance is a requirement for listed projects, and a public hearing is mandatory for most of them."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Ombudsman","q":"Which State was the first to establish the institution of Lokayukta, by legislation in 1971?","o":["Maharashtra","Rajasthan","Karnataka","Gujarat"],"a":0,"e":"The Administrative Reforms Commission (1966-70) recommended the institutions of Lokpal and Lokayukta. The Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013 requires every State to establish a Lokayukta."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Natural Justice","q":"In Ridge v. Baldwin (1964), the House of Lords revived the application of natural justice to:","o":["only the decisions of the courts of law of record in India","only the decisions of a legislative nature made by Parliament","administrative decisions affecting a person's rights, like dismissal","only the decisions of a purely ministerial type, and no others in practice"],"a":2,"e":"The Chief Constable of Brighton had been dismissed without a hearing, and the decision was held void. The case overcame the narrow view that natural justice applies only to judicial functions."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Discretion","q":"In Padfield v. Minister of Agriculture (1968), the House of Lords held that a discretionary power conferred by statute:","o":["must be exercised to promote the policy and objects of the Act","is absolute, and cannot be reviewed by any court of law","may be exercised on any ground that the Minister thinks fit","may be refused without giving any reasons in every case"],"a":0,"e":"The Minister had refused to refer a complaint to a committee for reasons unrelated to the Act. There is no unfettered discretion in public law."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"In Indian Council for Enviro-Legal Action v. Union of India (1996), the Bichhri case, the Supreme Court applied the polluter pays principle to hold that the polluting industries:","o":["need pay only a fine fixed by the Pollution Control Board","need pay only if the pollution is shown to be intentional","are liable only to the extent of their paid-up share capital","must bear the cost of remedying the damage to the environment"],"a":3,"e":"The case involved toxic sludge from chemical plants in a village in Rajasthan. The Court held that remediation costs, and not only compensation to victims, are to be borne by the polluter."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"The Environment (Protection) Act, 1986, enacted after the Bhopal gas tragedy, was passed under which Article to implement the decisions of the Stockholm Conference (1972)?","o":["Article 48A","Article 21","Article 253","Article 51A"],"a":2,"e":"Article 253 gives Parliament the power to legislate to implement international decisions. Section 3 of the Act empowers the Central Government to take measures to protect the environment."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Labour Law","q":"In Bangalore Water Supply and Sewerage Board v. A. Rajappa (1978), the Supreme Court laid down the 'triple test' to determine an 'industry' under the Industrial Disputes Act, which is:","o":["systematic activity organised by employer-employee cooperation to produce goods or services","profit motive, private ownership and a minimum of fifty workmen employed in the unit","charitable purpose, public ownership and a licence under the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947","government control, a ban on strikes, and a fixed minimum wage for all workers"],"a":0,"e":"The Court gave a wide meaning to 'industry', covering hospitals, universities and professions. A seven-judge reference in 2005 on the correctness of the test remains pending. (Verify the present status.)"},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Social Legislation","q":"Under Section 3 of the Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961, giving or taking dowry is punishable with imprisonment of not less than:","o":["seven years, and fine of ₹25,000 or the value of the dowry received","one year, and fine of ₹5,000 or the value of the dowry received","three years, and fine of ₹10,000 or the value of the dowry received","five years, and fine of ₹15,000 or the value of the dowry, if more"],"a":3,"e":"Demanding dowry directly or indirectly is punishable under Section 4 with imprisonment of six months to two years. Section 8A places the burden of proof on the person who takes or demands dowry."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Presidential Reference","q":"In its opinion on the Presidential Reference under Article 143 on timelines for Governors and the President to act on Bills (2025), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["Governors must decide every Bill within one month, failing which assent is deemed","courts cannot fix rigid timelines or recognise 'deemed assent' for Bills","Governors have no power to withhold assent in any situation","the President must decide within three months, failing which the Bill lapses"],"a":1,"e":"The reference followed the April 2025 ruling in State of Tamil Nadu v. Governor. The Court indicated that prolonged inaction may be subject to limited judicial review. (Verify the details of the opinion.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judicial Accountability","q":"Under the Judges (Inquiry) Act, 1968, the removal of a High Court judge for proved misbehaviour requires:","o":["a recommendation by the Chief Justice of India accepted by the President","a vote of the State Legislature, followed by the Governor's order","an address by each House, by a special majority, presented to the President","a finding by a Supreme Court bench, which the President executes"],"a":2,"e":"Articles 124(4) and 217(1)(b) apply. A motion needs 100 members of the Lok Sabha or 50 of the Rajya Sabha, followed by an inquiry committee. Justice V. Ramaswami's motion failed in 1993. (Verify the recent in-house inquiry into a Delhi High Court judge.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Personal Laws","q":"In 2024, which State enacted a Uniform Civil Code law that, among other things, requires registration of live-in relationships?","o":["Gujarat","Uttarakhand","Goa","Assam"],"a":1,"e":"The Uttarakhand Uniform Civil Code Act, 2024 excludes Scheduled Tribes. Goa has a Portuguese-era common civil code which pre-dates its accession. (Verify the status of implementation.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constituent Assembly","q":"The Objectives Resolution, which formed the basis of the Preamble, was moved in the Constituent Assembly by:","o":["Rajendra Prasad, on 9 December 1946","Jawaharlal Nehru, on 13 December 1946","B.R. Ambedkar, on 13 December 1946","Sardar Patel, on 22 January 1947"],"a":1,"e":"The Assembly adopted the Resolution on 22 January 1947. The Constituent Assembly first met on 9 December 1946."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Legal Education","q":"The first National Law School in India, which started in 1987 as a model of legal education reform, is the National Law School of India University at:","o":["Bhopal","Kolkata","Hyderabad","Bengaluru"],"a":3,"e":"NLSIU, founded under a Karnataka Act of 1986, was led by N.R. Madhava Menon. NLU Delhi, which conducts AILET, was set up in 2008 under the National Law University, Delhi Act, 2007."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constituent Assembly","q":"Who served as the Constitutional Adviser to the Constituent Assembly and prepared the initial draft of the Constitution in 1947?","o":["N. Gopalaswami Ayyangar","B.N. Rau","K.M. Munshi","Alladi Krishnaswami Ayyar"],"a":1,"e":"The Drafting Committee, chaired by B.R. Ambedkar, was set up on 29 August 1947 to examine the draft. S.N. Mukherjee was the chief draftsman."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Forests","q":"In T.N. Godavarman Thirumulpad v. Union of India (1996), the Supreme Court held that the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 applies to:","o":["only forests formally notified under the Indian Forest Act, 1927 in practice","only forests that are more than ten hectares in extent","only forests owned by the Central or the State Government","all areas that are forests in the dictionary sense, regardless of owner"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 amendment (now the Van (Sanrakshan Evam Samvardhan) Adhiniyam) limits the Act's scope to notified and recorded forests, and the Court directed in 2024 that the dictionary meaning continue meanwhile. (Verify the final outcome.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Animal Law","q":"In August 2025, in a suo motu case on stray dogs in Delhi-NCR, the Supreme Court modified an earlier direction and ordered that:","o":["stray dogs be culled by municipal bodies wherever they are found","feeding of stray dogs be allowed freely at any public place","sterilised and vaccinated dogs be released, except rabid or aggressive ones","all stray dogs be permanently kept in shelters across the whole country"],"a":2,"e":"The Court also directed that feeding be done only at designated places. The directions were extended to States and Union Territories. (Verify the later developments.)"}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Supreme Court in Sita Soren (2024) held that parliamentary privilege does not protect bribery by legislators, overruling P.V. Narasimha Rao. Discuss the balance between parliamentary privilege, accountability and the rule of law.","o":["Constitutional basis: Articles 105 and 194; privilege protects free debate and is not a personal shield.","Case law: P.V. Narasimha Rao (1998), Raja Ram Pal (2007) and Sita Soren (2024).","Reasoning: bribery is complete on acceptance and is not essential to a legislator's functions; privilege is not above criminal law.","Counter-views: possible misuse of prosecution against opposition legislators; need to safeguard free speech in the House.","Conclusion: privilege must be read with constitutional morality and anti-corruption law."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"Critically examine whether the new criminal laws (BNS, BNSS and BSA) strike an adequate balance between victim protection and the rights of the accused. Refer to at least three provisions.","o":["Background: replacement of IPC, CrPC and Evidence Act from 1 July 2024.","Victim-oriented provisions: Sections 69 and 70 BNS, victim compensation, free treatment, progress updates within 90 days (BNSS).","Accused-oriented concerns: extended police custody, trial in absentia, wide organised crime and terrorism definitions.","Technology and fairness: audio-video recording of search and seizure, electronic summons, electronic evidence certificate under BSA.","Conclusion: many reforms are progressive, but safeguards, implementation and judicial oversight decide the balance. (Verify section numbers against the statutes.)"]}]};
